WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.004 --> 00:00:15.146
It was neither the season nor the hour when the park had visitors, and it is likely that

2
00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:20.008
the young lady, who was seated on one of the benches at the side of the walk, had

3
00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:25.291
merely obeyed a sudden impulse to sit for a while and enjoy the prospect of coming spring.

4
00:00:26.291 --> 00:00:28.472
She rested there, pensive and still.

5
00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:34.708
A certain melancholy in her expression must have been of recent birth, for it had not yet altered

6
00:00:34.748 --> 00:00:41.354
the fine and youthful lines of her cheek, nor tamed the mischievous though resolute curve of her lips.

7
00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:46.898
A tall young man came striding through the park along the path near which she sat.

8
00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:50.301
Behind him tagged along a boy carrying a suitcase.

9
00:00:51.242 --> 00:00:56.005
At the sight of the young lady, the man's face changed to red and back to pale again.

10
00:00:57.026 --> 00:01:01.457
He watched her face as he drew nearer, with hope and anxiety mingled on his own.

11
00:01:02.518 --> 00:01:07.400
He passed within a few yards of her, but he saw no evidence that she was aware of

12
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.081
his presence or existence.

13
00:01:10.882 --> 00:01:16.485
Some fifty yards further on, he suddenly stopped and sat on a bench at one side.

14
00:01:17.326 --> 00:01:21.908
The boy dropped the suitcase and stared at him with wondering, shrewd eyes.

15
00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:25.550
The young man took out his handkerchief and wiped his brow.

16
00:01:26.351 --> 00:01:31.077
It was a good handkerchief, a good brow, and the young man was good to look at.

17
00:01:31.837 --> 00:01:36.279
He said to the boy: "I want you to take a message to that young lady on that

18
00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:41.902
bench. Tell her I am on my way to the station, to leave for San Francisco, where I

19
00:01:41.942 --> 00:01:44.823
shall join that Alaska moose-hunting expedition.

20
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:50.966
Tell her that since she has commanded me neither to speak nor to write to her, I take

21
00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:55.328
this means of making one last appeal to her sense of justice, for the sake of what has

22
00:01:55.388 --> 00:02:02.683
been. Tell her that to condemn and discard one who has not deserved such treatment, without giving him

23
00:02:02.743 --> 00:02:08.025
her reasons or a chance to explain, is contrary to her nature as I believe it to be.

24
00:02:09.025 --> 00:02:15.168
Tell her that I have, to a certain degree, disobeyed her orders, in the hope that she may

25
00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.448
yet be willing to see justice done.

26
00:02:18.229 --> 00:02:19.909
Go, and tell her that."

27
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.771
The young man dropped a half-dollar into the boy's hand.

28
00:02:24.431 --> 00:02:29.766
The boy looked at him for a moment with bright eyes out of a dirty, intelligent face, and

29
00:02:29.806 --> 00:02:31.067
then set off at a run.

30
00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:35.990
He approached the lady on the bench a little doubtfully, but unembarrassed.

31
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:40.672
He touched the brim of the old plaid cap perched on the back of his head.

32
00:02:41.473 --> 00:02:45.315
The lady looked at him coolly, without prejudice or favor.

33
00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:51.398
"Lady," he said, "that guy on the other bench sent me to give you a song and dance.

34
00:02:52.199 --> 00:02:56.293
If you don't know him, and he's bothering you, just say the word, and I'll have a cop

35
00:02:56.353 --> 00:02:57.534
over here in three minutes.

36
00:02:58.574 --> 00:03:02.337
If you do know him, and he's telling the truth, I'll tell you what he wanted me to

37
00:03:02.377 --> 00:03:02.797
tell you."

38
00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:05.819
The young lady showed a faint interest.

39
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:07.720
"A song and dance!"

40
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.803
she said, in a sweet voice that seemed to clothe her words in a barely noticeable layer of

41
00:03:13.963 --> 00:03:18.446
irony. "A new idea—like a singing telegram, I suppose.

42
00:03:19.486 --> 00:03:24.937
I used to know the gentleman who sent you, so I think it will hardly be necessary to

43
00:03:24.977 --> 00:03:25.717
call the police.

44
00:03:26.778 --> 00:03:30.679
You may execute your song and dance, but do not sing too loudly.

45
00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:36.081
It is a little early yet for open-air performances, and we might attract attention."

46
00:03:36.101 --> 00:03:41.784
"Aw," said the boy, with a shrug down the length of him, "you know what I mean, lady.

47
00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:46.485
It's not a real song and dance, it's just an expression.

48
00:03:47.306 --> 00:03:51.668
He told me to tell you he's got all his stuff packed into that suitcase to leave for

49
00:03:51.708 --> 00:03:52.528
San Francisco.

50
00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:55.814
Then, he's going to shoot some snowbirds in the Klondike.

51
00:03:56.494 --> 00:04:00.357
He says you told him not to send any more notes or come to see you in your

52
00:04:00.397 --> 00:04:03.439
garden, and he's using me to set the record straight.

53
00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:09.003
He says you judged him, and never even gave him a chance to change your mind.

54
00:04:09.543 --> 00:04:11.865
He says you left him, and never said why."

55
00:04:12.465 --> 00:04:16.608
The slightly awakened interest in the young lady's eyes did not lessen.

56
00:04:17.248 --> 00:04:23.853
Perhaps it was caused by either the originality or the audacity of the snowbird hunter, who was bypassing

57
00:04:23.913 --> 00:04:27.716
her commands against the ordinary modes of communication in this way.

58
00:04:28.497 --> 00:04:34.722
She fixed her eye on a cheerless statue standing in the disheveled park, and spoke into the transmitter:

59
00:04:34.742 --> 00:04:39.967
"Tell the gentleman that I need not repeat to him a description of my ideals.

60
00:04:40.768 --> 00:04:43.470
He knows what they have been and what they still are.

61
00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:50.096
So far as they relate to this case, absolute loyalty and truth are the ones paramount.

62
00:04:50.784 --> 00:04:55.485
Tell him that I have studied my own heart as well as one can, and I know its

63
00:04:55.545 --> 00:04:58.006
weaknesses as well as I do its needs.

64
00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:02.187
That is why I decline to hear his pleas, whatever they may be.

65
00:05:02.967 --> 00:05:08.189
I did not condemn him because of a rumor or doubtful evidence, and that is why I made

66
00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:08.849
no charge.

67
00:05:09.649 --> 00:05:15.371
But, since he persists in hearing what he already well knows, you may convey the matter.

68
00:05:15.391 --> 00:05:21.197
"Tell him that I entered the greenhouse that evening from the rear, to cut a rose for my

69
00:05:21.237 --> 00:05:26.660
mother. Tell him I saw him and Miss Ashburton beneath the pink oleander.

70
00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:33.763
The scene was pretty, but the pose and juxtaposition were too eloquent and evident to require explanation.

71
00:05:34.564 --> 00:05:39.927
I left the greenhouse, and at the same time, the rose and my illusions of ideal love.

72
00:05:40.527 --> 00:05:43.068
You may carry that song and dance to your boss."

73
00:05:43.088 --> 00:05:45.710
"I'm not clear on one word, lady.

74
00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:49.412
Jux—jux—explain that, will you?"

75
00:05:50.092 --> 00:05:58.199
"Juxtaposition—or you may call it proximity—or, if you like, being rather too near for one maintaining the position

76
00:05:58.319 --> 00:05:59.039
of an ideal."

77
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.242
The gravel spun from beneath the boy's feet.

78
00:06:02.882 --> 00:06:04.163
He stood by the other bench.

79
00:06:04.884 --> 00:06:07.506
The man's eyes interrogated him hungrily.

80
00:06:08.127 --> 00:06:11.870
The boy's were shining with the impersonal eagerness of the translator.

81
00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:17.214
"The lady says that she knows that girls are often easy to fool when a fellow comes along

82
00:06:17.294 --> 00:06:22.366
telling them fairy tales and trying to make up, and that's why she won't listen to any nonsense.

83
00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:26.850
She says she caught you in the act, hugging some girl in the hothouse.

84
00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:32.194
She stopped by to get some flowers and you were squeezing some other girl really tight.

85
00:06:32.854 --> 00:06:35.677
She says it looked cute all right, but it made her sick.

86
00:06:36.317 --> 00:06:38.539
She says you'd better get going to catch your train."

87
00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.024
The young man gave a low whistle and his eyes flashed with a sudden understanding.

88
00:06:44.864 --> 00:06:49.228
His hand flew to the inside pocket of his coat, and drew out a handful of letters.

89
00:06:50.044 --> 00:06:55.667
Selecting one, he handed it to the boy, following it with a silver dollar from his vest pocket.

90
00:06:55.687 --> 00:07:00.410
"Give that letter to the lady," he said, "and ask her to read it.

91
00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.551
Tell her that it should explain the situation.

92
00:07:04.332 --> 00:07:09.394
Tell her that if she had mingled a little trust with her conception of the ideal, much heartache

93
00:07:09.474 --> 00:07:10.495
might have been avoided.

94
00:07:11.355 --> 00:07:15.057
Tell her that the loyalty she prizes so much has never wavered.

95
00:07:15.718 --> 00:07:17.459
Tell her I am waiting for an answer."

96
00:07:17.999 --> 00:07:19.740
The messenger stood before the lady.

97
00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.721
"The guy says he's been dumped for no good reason.

98
00:07:23.261 --> 00:07:27.782
He says he's not a bad guy, and lady, you should read that letter, and I'll bet you

99
00:07:27.822 --> 00:07:29.163
he's telling the truth, all right."

100
00:07:29.863 --> 00:07:33.624
The young lady unfolded the letter, somewhat doubtfully, and read it.

101
00:07:34.664 --> 00:07:35.384
Dear Dr.

102
00:07:35.544 --> 00:07:41.186
Arnold: I want to thank you for your most kind and opportune aid to my daughter last Friday

103
00:07:41.306 --> 00:07:47.028
evening, when she was overcome by an attack of her old heart trouble in the greenhouse at Mrs.

104
00:07:47.088 --> 00:07:48.108
Waldron's reception.

105
00:07:48.992 --> 00:07:53.894
Had you not been near to catch her as she fell and to render proper attention, we might

106
00:07:53.914 --> 00:07:54.534
have lost her.

107
00:07:55.315 --> 00:07:58.456
I would be glad if you would call and check on the treatment of her case.

108
00:07:59.176 --> 00:08:05.899
Gratefully yours, Robert Ashburton The young lady refolded the letter, and handed it to the boy.

109
00:08:05.919 --> 00:08:08.841
"The fellow wants an answer," said the messenger.

110
00:08:08.861 --> 00:08:10.401
"What's your reply?"

111
00:08:11.342 --> 00:08:16.224
The lady's eyes suddenly flashed on him, bright, smiling, and wet.

112
00:08:17.024 --> 00:08:23.978
"Tell that guy on the other bench," she said, with a happy, trembling laugh, "that his girl wants

113
00:08:24.038 --> 00:08:24.218
him."
